  "solution": {
    "text": "<p>When calcium hydroxide is reacted with crystals of ammonium chloride, the products formed as calcium chloride, ammonia and water.</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>Ca</mi><mo>(</mo><mi>OH</mi><msub><mo>)</mo><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>+</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><msub><mi>NH</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mi>Cl</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>&#8594;</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><msub><mi>CaCl</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>+</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>NH</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>+</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mn>2</mn><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi></math></p><p>When ammonia is reacted with water and excess carbon dioxide, it forms&nbsp;ammonium hydrogen carbonate which on passing through with saturated NaCl&nbsp;solution forms hydrogen carbonate.</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>NH</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>+</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>+</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><msub><mi>CO</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>&#8594;</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><msub><mi>NH</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><msub><mi>HCO</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>NH</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><msub><mi>HCO</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>+</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>NaCl</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>&#8594;</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><msub><mi>NaHCO</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>&#8595;</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>+</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><msub><mi>NH</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mi>Cl</mi></math></p><p>So, the compound A, B, and C are&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>C</mi><mi>a</mi><mo>(</mo><mi>O</mi><mi>H</mi><msub><mo>)</mo><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>N</mi><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>N</mi><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mi>H</mi><mi>C</mi><msub><mi>O</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></math>&nbsp;respectively.</p><p>&nbsp;</p>",
